                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e80fca42-ab2b-3c68-a846-c5b6242ec60b",
                        "entry_id": "bc7dd8d4-8e97-3eaa-b647-a1ecc3f264ff",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Relatif à un facteur."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d4974d00-c97b-49c2-b7a0-b6cc50e81f30",
                                "sense_id": "e80fca42-ab2b-3c68-a846-c5b6242ec60b",
                                "text": "un nombre premier factoriel est un nombre qui est égal à une factorielle plus 1 ou moins 1 et qui est aussi un nombre premier."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6fcbbd53-0710-4999-bf5b-e406081f8d55",
                                "sense_id": "e80fca42-ab2b-3c68-a846-c5b6242ec60b",
                                "text": "Tout élément d’un anneau factoriel se décompose en un produit d’un élément inversible et d’éléments irréductibles."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "1b4c54f4-4163-4d58-b4b8-ca7bffbee39c",
                        "source_entry_id": "bc7dd8d4-8e97-3eaa-b647-a1ecc3f264ff",
                        "source_sense_id": "e80fca42-ab2b-3c68-a846-c5b6242ec60b",
                        "target_lang_word": "factorial",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "factorial",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "bf2e97a9-08a7-3a17-a4fe-075ae7b4133c",
                                        "entry_id": "9ab7b0e5-0317-3357-8602-457c1b5bb716",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The result of multiplying a given number of consecutive integers from 1 to the given number. In equations, it is symbolized by an exclamation mark (!). For example, 5! = 1 × 2 × 3 × 4 × 5 = 120."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f280ebdc-4c62-443b-959d-4ba708ff6f65",
                                                "sense_id": "bf2e97a9-08a7-3a17-a4fe-075ae7b4133c",
                                                "text": "The expression #123;#125;#95;nC#95;r means the number of combinations of n things taken r at a time. It is also written #92;binomnr, and is equal to#92;frac#123;n(n-1)(n-2)(n-3)#92;cdots#92;cdots(n-r#43;1)#125;#123;r#33;#125;or n#33;#47;r#33;(n-r)#33;, in which r#33; is read \"factorial r\" and denotes the product of all the integral numbers from 1 to r inclusive."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b5ad7d64-2a7a-4df7-8c51-6596d4c4ea59",
                                                "sense_id": "bf2e97a9-08a7-3a17-a4fe-075ae7b4133c",
                                                "text": "This playing around with consecutive integers reminded one of the students of factorials, and he asked about products of integers, which I said could be expressed similarly as#92;prod#95;#123;k#61;1#125;ⁿk#61;1#92;cdot 2#92;cdot 3#92;cdotsn.But what does this equal?\nWell, of course we can say that it is equal to n factorial and write#92;prod#95;#123;k#61;1#125;ⁿk#61;n#33; and then use our calculators, but inventing a new symbol like 'factorial' feels like an admission of defeat!"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "factorial",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "182226f5-b141-3413-bc23-f4f41c0525eb",
                                        "entry_id": "01dbf3c9-afcc-3d43-b2fb-3033e7a52f29",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or pertaining to a factor or factorial."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c71ce6ea-b14c-44e8-a22d-e5deff365d9b",
                                                "sense_id": "182226f5-b141-3413-bc23-f4f41c0525eb",
                                                "text": "I shall denote, as usual, the factorial product of the numbers up to n by n#33;, but I have found it necessary to introduce a separate notation for the products of successive even or odd numbers. I consequently definen#33;#33;#61;n#92;cdot(n-2)#33;#33;.Starting with1#33;#33;#61;1,#92;quad 2#33;#33;#61;2,it follows that, for positive values of n,n#33;#33;#61;n#92;cdotn-2#92;cdotn-4#92;cdots,where the last factor is either 1 or 2, according as n is odd or even."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fcb2e059-e7f2-418e-9e4e-efaa4753e5ca",
                                                "sense_id": "182226f5-b141-3413-bc23-f4f41c0525eb",
                                                "text": "The definition of addition is an example of definition by induction, sometimes called recursive definition. As another example of this, consider the following inductive definition of the factorial function.\nWe define n#33; for n#92;in#92;mathbbN by the following two properties,\n(i) 1#33; is defined to be 1,\n(ii) for all k#92;in#92;mathbbN, we define (k#43;1)#33; to be (k#43;1)#92;timesk#33;."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "89dcb1bc-b93d-3265-ac46-657d7b1a35ff",
                                        "entry_id": "01dbf3c9-afcc-3d43-b2fb-3033e7a52f29",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or pertaining to a factor, a kind of business agent."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1217b4fa-b04b-4e6c-864b-c7bc6c93147d",
                                                "sense_id": "89dcb1bc-b93d-3265-ac46-657d7b1a35ff",
                                                "text": "The latter sold the goods to a customer who was cashier to certain creditors of the agents without disclosing the factorial capacity in which they acted."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b5d8c8c3-39be-3007-9398-e2c256ce99b9",
                                        "entry_id": "01dbf3c9-afcc-3d43-b2fb-3033e7a52f29",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or pertaining to a factory."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "dated"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
